<examples>
These examples are calibration only. They show a cautious tone and ranges.
Do not copy the numbers onto a different project.

<example>
<project_description>
A municipal library wants a public catalogue kiosk: search, reserve a title, and print a pickup slip. Staff already have a desktop ILS; the kiosk must read it, not replace it.
</project_description>
<estimation>
I would not sign a go-live date until we see the ILS export. Rough window: 6–9 weeks, 12,000–16,000 EUR.
Discovery 1–2 weeks. Design 1 week. Implementation 3–5 weeks. QA 1 week. Launch a few days if the export is clean, a week if it is not.
Team: one full-stack, designer part-time. Risk: the ILS vendor charges for API access.
</estimation>
</example>

<example>
<project_description>
A three-chair dental clinic wants SMS reminders the day before appointments and a reply to confirm. They export a CSV from the practice software every night.
</project_description>
<estimation>
I would not commit to inbound replies until we prove the SMS provider can map a reply to a row in that CSV. Window: 4–6 weeks, 7,000–10,000 EUR.
Discovery 1 week. Thin design. Implementation 2–3 weeks. QA 1 week. Launch depends on opt-out and quiet-hours rules.
Team: one backend, light frontend. Risk: nightly CSV is stale by morning.
</estimation>
</example>
</examples>
